::ENG2_U06_L02_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which oral outline order is clearest for listeners?{
~A. Evidence, closing, opening, random fact, claim.#Incorrect. This order is confusing.
=B. Opening, claim, evidence, reasoning, closing.#Correct. This order helps listeners follow the speaker's point.
~C. Closing, claim, opening, evidence, reasoning.#Incorrect. Starting with the closing weakens organization.
~D. Random facts, claim, no explanation, ending.#Incorrect. Random facts and no reasoning do not support clear oral communication.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which source detail is most credible evidence?{
~A. My friend says hallways are bad.#Incorrect. This is a personal comment without source support.
~B. Everybody knows students wait around.#Incorrect. This is vague and unsupported.
~C. I think a study space sounds nice.#Incorrect. This is an opinion, not evidence.
=D. A survey of 120 students reports that 68 students remain on campus after dismissal at least one day each week.#Correct. This is specific evidence from a stated survey.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Which sentence is reasoning?{
=A. This evidence matters because a supervised study space would make waiting time safer and more productive.#Correct. This explains why the evidence supports the claim.
~B. Source A surveyed 120 students.#Incorrect. This is evidence, not reasoning.
~C. The handbook mentions supervised locations.#Incorrect. This is evidence from a source.
~D. The source is called Student Survey Summary.#Incorrect. This is a source label, not reasoning.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] What is the central idea of the source set?{
~A. Students should block hallways after school.#Incorrect. The handbook says students should not block hallways.
~B. Surveys are never useful.#Incorrect. The survey provides relevant information.
=C. Many students remain after dismissal, so supervised waiting or study space is a reasonable school concern.#Correct. This central idea reflects both sources.
~D. Students should leave campus immediately in every case.#Incorrect. The sources describe students who remain on campus.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which closing statement best fits the oral presentation?{
~A. That is all, and I do not care what happens.#Incorrect. This is disrespectful and weak.
=B. A supervised study space would help the school organize after-school waiting time while supporting student learning.#Correct. This closing reinforces the claim and reasoning.
~C. Students wait, handbook, survey, goodbye.#Incorrect. This is not a clear closing statement.
~D. The school should do random things after dismissal.#Incorrect. This is vague and unsupported.
}
